### 3.2 Overview of Common Mapping Methods
 
The `dict`, `collections.defaultdict`, and `collections.OrderedDict` classes all share a large common API. The most important methods to know:
 
| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `d[key]` | Returns value for `key`; raises `KeyError` if missing |
| `d.get(key, default=None)` | Returns value or `default` if key is missing |
| `d.setdefault(key, default)` | Returns `d[key]` if it exists; otherwise sets `d[key] = default` and returns `default` |
| `d[key] = value` | Sets or overwrites `key` |
| `del d[key]` | Removes `key`; raises `KeyError` if missing |
| `key in d` | Membership test (`__contains__`) |
| `d.keys()` | Returns a view of all keys |
| `d.values()` | Returns a view of all values |
| `d.items()` | Returns a view of all `(key, value)` pairs |
| `d.update(other)` | Merges `other` (dict or iterable of pairs) into `d` in place |
| `d.pop(key, default)` | Removes and returns `d[key]`, or `default` if missing |
| `d.popitem()` | Removes and returns the last inserted `(key, value)` pair (LIFO) |
| `d.clear()` | Removes all items |
| `d.copy()` | Returns a shallow copy |
 
> **`dict.get()` vs `dict[key]`:** Use `.get()` whenever a missing key is a normal, expected case. Use `d[key]` when a missing key is a bug and you want to fail loudly.
 
